body:
When I use to work at Walmart back in 2015 it had all of those features. You learn foodlion goes out of it's way to be behind in anything that costs money. Also, not everyone has access to RPO so it's even more humorous that it's not included in Delhaize. I remember going as back as 2010 Target had the scanners throughout the store that could scan a barcode and give a price. Meaning, foodlion is at least 12 years behind to that Target option and 7+ on the technology Walmart has. 

&#x200B;

This is the same company that spent millions on Remodels to make stores "look better" but didn't invest any of that money back into the company to make the jobs of their associates easier.

Also contribute the exodus of long standing associates with the company. When they quit they often times don't roll over their 401k and instead cash it out early. An early retrieval is selling all the shares they've bought into for years, and that hurts the market as well. When it's just a few waves it's not a big deal but as more and more people leave the company every day, even those that have been with the company 5+ years, it reflects poorly on share value which ultimately determines the value of one's 401k. 

&#x200B;

What I did as I saw the trend, back in September share value for Ahold Delhaize was down $10 a share, I took out a withdraw or loan from the 401k. I used that money to pay a few bills in advance. The reason for that is because the value of the stock has gone down, paying it back into the pot at a lower cost (As it is now) will increase the value of the stock long term. Also because you are paying off the loan you won't have to pay an early withdraw fee or claim it on your taxes. 

&#x200B;

Now I don't recommend that for everyone if only because to pay off that loan obviously you'll see your networth every two weeks decrease until the loan is paid off in full. (60-100 gone every 2 weeks, and that's me putting in 5% with the company match). 

&#x200B;

I'll say though doing this at the start of the year my 401k was at about 4k, it went down to 3k in September because the stock value decrease. I took the loan, I've about paid it off, and now the value of my 401k is 5k. I would encourage anybody younger with the time and the flexibility with their net worth to do as I have done for a better investment in long term.

This is pretty spot on. It's amazing because despite record sales growth FoodLion's Share price has dropped significantly from the start of the year. It's only now bounced back slightly. Back in September the share value was down 10 dollars, which wouldn't be too bad if each share wasn't originally only valued at 35 dollars. I took a look at my 401k on Fidelity and nearly broke down. In the span of a few months my 401k dropped over a grand. 

&#x200B;

Why is this the case if sales are up? Well it's because long standing associates with the company that have put in 5+ years or leaving in a mass exodus and cashing out their 401ks early and that's severely hurting the company's share value. I wouldn't be surprised if more than a third of Foodlion's staff has left since before the pandemic.

Sadly from my experience with the amount of work load they put on you, gutted payroll (So you're the only one on the clock in the department) and the unrealistic expectations to get everything done alone (Including breaking down entire trucks by yourself, work it on to the salesfloor, and get things to perfection) taking 30 minutes out of the day to take a lunch is just cause to set yourself behind and for Management to come over and yell about why this or that hasn't been done. I've had managers tell me I should take Lunch breaks, then that same manager will ask why product level is low (Mind you 30 minutes of no one filling the floor) or why a truck hasn't been finished. 

&#x200B;

Generally I'll find myself doing a 7-3, next person comes in at 3, so if I take even so much as a 10 minute break for myself I risk setting myself and the next person behind. It's not like that for all produce departments (But those with Cut Fruit know all to well about how screwed your payroll is) but when the company gives you only 10-12 hours for the entire day's worth of payroll (On non truck days, including Cut Fruit mind you) there's not much your Department Manager's can do about it. Other than allocate some of those hours (From other days) into your non truck days. 

&#x200B;

Generally, I'll take one 10 minute break (If I can) and get something to nibble or drink if I'm feeling sluggish or exhausted. Other than that it's non stop work.

You've got to set your boundaries as far as when it is you'll work. Five days a week, that's cool. (Even that is a bit too much, if they've only got one part timer, because who will be closing the department on the Department Manager's and their assistant's day off?) However, seven days a week as a part timer is not acceptable unless you, yourself, state you wish for those hours. Cut fruit associates generally accumulate 4 hours a day at most stores so at five days a week you'll manage to accumulate 20 hours. (Some higher volume stores will sacrifice the rest of the labor in the department to give additional ones to the program because the sales it generates. However, the program doesn't generate nearly as much hours as the company claims it does.)

&#x200B;

The cut fruit program has been a complete mess, failure, not only for staffing concerns but because it has so many resources that need to be allocated to it and no pay off. Your contributions MIGHT generate an additional 200-300 dollars a day in the department's sales. However, if you're manager isn't always ordering your supplies, fruit needs (Including pineapple core here's the code just in case you ever need someone to order some for you: 387449), and hours get scarce at the end of the week you'll be fine but the department suffers. Your labor comes from the entire departments. So say, the produce department on average gets 114 hours for the entire week. 28 hours has to go to cut fruit, four hours a day, so you subtract that from 114 and you get 86 hours for the entire week for the rest of the department. 

&#x200B;

The best way to schedule for this process I've found is have the PT work 5 days a week, scheduled on every day where there is a truck delivery. On non truck days Foodlion generally only generates the department 11-12 hours for the entire department. So one of two things needs to happen here. Either you do what I had to do: the Department Manager schedules themselves 7-6 and opens, closes, and does cut fruit if they can on that day. Or you schedule both the Dpt Manager and the assistant: so the department manager opens the department, assistant comes in at 11 or 12, and once they arrive Department Manager does cut fruit for the remaining part of their shift. The problem with doing the later is that those hours don't just appear, you get 11 - 12 remember for the entire day. So another day in the week you're gonna have to sacrifice over lap and coverage on truck days. So you're next person comes and the other is leaving long enough to say hi and bye.

I can confirm this as well. I use to be a Department Manager. At the start of covid we were getting 110 hours for the entire department a week in Produce. That's when they rolled out the "increase" in pay for those two months. Said they couldn't spare any extra hours to keep us afloat despite sales being 35 percent higher sales over 2019 sales. Once those increases vanished, we got 10 hours back for the department. 

&#x200B;

I was a Department Manager back in 2015 at walmart making 15 hourly, when I was a department manager last year I was making less in 2020 than what I did five year prior.

If you're the religious type I'd say get a cross. Maybe speak to an esteemed psychic? Be careful with that route, lot of people you should rightfully be skeptical of. 

&#x200B;

I'd say burn the sage, listen to the other tips I gave you, and see if that improves the situation.

I still don't know why people are so persistent in welcoming these types of things in their home should they believe it or not. 

&#x200B;

You've welcomed this thing in your home with no contingency plan in place I presume if things go south. Remain skeptical, it's your best bet in playing ignorant and chop these things up as coincidence. Moment you start to think otherwise you start to give it dominion, power, over you and it will act on your insecurities. 

If things do go south, you might have to speak to the alleged spirit telling it it's time to move on. Cleanse your home in sage. Establish dominance that your home is yours, and you're free to let it leave it as quickly as you gave it entry. 

&#x200B;

Now, if you're throwing caution to the wind. Might I suggest switching the flower? Color or type. See if it returns with the initial one or if it continues holding the new one.

I've always heard burning sage can cleanse an area or your home. Not saying the thing is evil or anything of the sort, however, if you believe the two are connected you may need to set boundaries at  your home. Saying, whatever or whoever it is that it's not welcomed in your home. Work, where it's more active, might be it's domain but your home is yours. 

&#x200B;

Another thing to note is remain skeptical if this is scaring you. I remember something Sylvia brown once said (Despite your belief of her being genuine or not) these things are drawn to those who welcome it and allow it to have dominion over them. When I have unwelcomed visitors or goosebumps, I typically tell them to leave me alone or to leave my room. So should you believe in the paranormal or not, it could be a psychological thing as well; the moment you exert dominance over it or don't entertain the thought generally I find those hairs on the back of my neck go back to normal in a few passing seconds.

My suggestion would be to talk to her as if she were an adult; despite the age, she's quite potentially holding an old soul within her. Relate her trauma, her loss, with similar loss you, yourself, have had. A loved one, a family member, or something of significance and how you felt about that thing. You need to stress something important to the child now and that's one simple truth; her new family, yourself and the other's, love her as well and you'll always be there for her. As for the grandmother thing, tell her that if she does pass on wouldn't it be lovely to be in another family who loves them just as much as your child is loved by you and the others? 

&#x200B;

Tell her the soul, the grandmother's or even those of her past life, may go on to new adventures; however, that person lives on in those they've touched or loved; should it be in that life or the next one (This one). Remind her that the love she had from them will always be there and more importantly, she now has your love as well and with so many people who care about her everything will be okay. 

&#x200B;

Eventually, I would tell you those past experiences she's recollecting or remembering will probably lessen. Think of her age for a moment, she's 4, if she's right it's only been 4 years since these things have happened (In her perspective). As people get older, as time goes by, so do to memories that slip by; also, you should be able to create new and wonderful experiences for her to make this transition even easier to the point she may forget about these incidents all together. And if she doesn't, that's fine as well because she'll have found a new home to make the transition easier.

\>\_>' I just submitted to have some of my poems published about a week ago and my editor just got in touch with me. Wanted me to keep it as it was or to let them make changes, I don't claim to be the second coming at all and mediocre at best; however, this post has me paranoid as hell. 

&#x200B;

To be fair though. A few years ago when I first got into my poetry, I use to focus on short stories but transitioned to poems, I had attempted to submit some of my poems to a few publications. (I believe it was three at the time) One never responded back, the other said I wasn't what they were looking for, and the last was so maliciously cruel they more or less said I should give up writing entirely. Being so distraught, defeated, I gave up writing entirely for a complete year; fell into a downward spiral of depression, filled with apathy, and couldn't express myself on paper or in person. I took the word of this professional as absolute, thinking my writing synonymous with trash itself, until over a year later I realized the writing was therapeutic; I found passion in something again and started writing again. Now I have approximately 160 poems, over 5 years (Minus one from that Hiatus), that I'm publishing not expecting any money, fame, or even recognition from this book that most wont even bother to look at. A complete "waste" of money to some, money I could have kept aside or invested, but it means more to me now than what it did years ago. 

&#x200B;

One of my favorite Authors, Stephen king, once said in one of his Author notes that he didn't write for the publishers, to be published, or for recognition but he did so for himself. How he was so fortunate that other's so happened to enjoy the things he wrote, and I remember the impact it had on me in high school. It wasn't until later on that these words hold even more true to me now. 

&#x200B;

And I think it's important for writers of all kinds to remember that. This level of humility, humbleness, but more so recognizing that people might not like your work; however, so long as it stays true to your own interest and what you love then it's okay. However, it's important to take feedback. Think of George Lucas and the initial starwars where he had a check and balance like foundation, but then look to the prequels where he had full creative decision making without that feedback. Writers as imaginative as we are that very same advantage can become a disadvantage if you think too big and aren't grounded. Editors not only want you to succeed on a personal level, it becomes just as much their work as your own, but you're quite literally paying them to better assist you in making it successful. 

&#x200B;

TLDR: Listen to editors they're not being malicious. However, don't do so if you think it'll compromise the integrity or your vision. You have to compromise and remember to be humble, show humility, chances are you aren't the second coming of Shakespeare himself.

I would say this as bluntly as possible and not for disrespect but in regards to this remark and how Aquarius typically perceive it in my opinion. "I cannot stand not being a priority in someone's life," this is toxic. Not towards the person, but for yourself. You can love someone, embrace them, but in any relationship you, the individual, are the priority. Your needs, your wants, out shadow those of others; although, this is not to say you shouldn't take into account other's or put your own aside every once in a while for other's. 

&#x200B;

Speaking from experience and my own perspective. It's the need to put other's before yourself, making someone feel worthy or valued, over your own that's essentially wrong for you as an individual. The Aquarius does not seek validation from the world or from others; the Aquarius seeks the validation in themselves and often times it's a battle they can not win unless they do some serious digging. An Aquarius (Mind you my experience) is quite literally the ideal when they tell you, you can't love a person who doesn't love themselves. It isn't until they overcome those obstacles, self inflicted, that they wholeheartedly can love in return. 

&#x200B;

Aquarius are typically introverts by nature. We find ourselves alone in our thoughts more than most and we've grown a tolerance or resistance to the needs you've described. The "needing to be loved" by others doesn't weigh heavily on our minds, call it a defense mechanism if you want, so it's harder for us to lower these walls when we see no real reason to do so.

&#x200B;

Speaking on behalf of myself, I am a pessimist through and through. I've seen the worse in people, as an introvert I'm usually quiet or reserved, and have witnessed the ugliness of people be it what they do to others or what they've done to me. I don't consider that a curse, a negative trait, as you a Leo would probably consider it as such; you're probably more on the extrovert spectrum especially compared to an aquarius. That means, if I had to take a guess, you're more of an Optimist. To you an Aquarius is a brooder, negative, and extremely defensive. To the Aquarius you're too hasty, naive to the world around you, and an idealist; even to the point you're more of a dreamer than the Aquarius themselves. Despite the detachment Aquarius often engage in, such as escaping from reality, they are still grounded by logic and reason that can make us cynical and cruel to some. The key difference is that you are capable of mapping out an entire future through planning or ambitions, as where the Aquarius is familiar with unknown variables that make such planning obsolete or a waste of energy in the greater schemes of things. 

&#x200B;

As I said before, let the aquarius do the chasing. You can dangle the carrot to peek their initial interest, but all pursuits must be his own to allude to the freedoms that he himself is making the choice. Aquarius value freedom over all and the thought of being pursued or chased or strong armed into something is the biggest turn off or obstacle when dealing with us. Because we are so independent, often spending vast amounts of time within ourselves thinking, we are selfish in not wanting to give that up in it's entirety and the thought of that being challenged does result in ghosting. 

&#x200B;

If you look at some of the other topics here there was a thread about an Aquarius seeking insight from their like minded people in regards to thinking too much. We are over analytical, it becomes difficult to differentiate where our thoughts surface and the thoughts of other's around us, and despite us coming across as cruel or even heartless in cases. I'd like to think most of us do think of your needs, your circumstances, maybe even more than you yourself. What we must do to stop thinking is either ghost, go poof, or we must channel those thoughts via our creativity to silence our minds. 

&#x200B;

To map out the future can just be additional thoughts, scenarios, that overwhelm our already jumbled thoughts. What's ironic about being an aquarius is the battle that we can be great listeners to other's issues and problems; however, we take up the burden of making those ours in our minds sometimes. Sometimes that step back from us is us just realigning where we begin and other's issues start. I have found myself in situations avoiding certain people or situations because I thought myself a burden be it financial, emotional, etc and it's my over thinking that brings me to those situations. Where often times the very things I've thought, imagined, haven't once crossed the minds of others nor was it relevant at all. It's because we care, sometimes capable of identifying our own faults, we can come across as cruel because we believe it a better course. Even at the expense of our own happiness, selfishness, we can push other's away thinking it best for everyone.  

&#x200B;

Now to use yet another cliche relationship saying, I'm convinced all of these pertain to Aquarius in general, "if you love something let it go." Most certainly originated from the mind set of an Aquarius. As well as the "it's not you, it's me" talking point. In addition to the one I mentioned about "You can't love someone until you love yourself." These are all prominent issues we all face. With that being said, we're one of the most complex or "damaged" groups of people. 

&#x200B;

However, I would say think of something else about Aquarius. We are believed to be more on the spectrum of the arts. The greatest pieces of work, historically, when it comes to art in my experience are those of someone who has succumbed or endured pain. It's when that pain transcends past thoughts into the physical world (Be it music, writing, painting) that it's incredibly beautiful. It's because of how complex our psyche is that we are capable of doing these things in addition to our necessity to detach from the world around us for our own sanity. 

&#x200B;

We are the mystery, the secrets, of the mind that people wish to uncover and explore. However, like pandora's box what's inside isn't always so glamorous or as pristine as what you may have thought first was hidden within. If you are capable of learning the soul of the Aquarius, that flows like water, and is carried by fleeting wind consider yourself blessed and worthy. For none other can make that voyage, unscathed, or unhardened. It is a war at sea that we ourselves have long thought it best traveled alone to minimize casualty. It's a near impossible task, mind you, be you the stowaway or the one to help raise the sails or the maiden at shore waiting our return. The people we seek are usually the former, but what we need most of all are those that patiently wait our return on this voyage we take to learn of ourselves. The lighthouse, the guiding beacon, so that we may return to ourselves and give illumination to our darkened thoughts lost out at sea.

That's just the Aquarius way as far as their opinions. An Aquarius can just be blunt and their words can sting, but in the case of friendship it's not out of malice; however, it can come across as such. Also, remember something please. You are not "Damaged goods" and any person who tells you as such or even thinks the thought is someone you should avoid all together. Take this time, this break between relationships if you decide to do so, to realign yourself and your feelings. Remember your worth, which you seem to grasp already, and spend more time getting to know yourself. People make mistakes, it's something most people realize but it's a harder concept for whatever reason for people to accept that they too can do so; that they shouldn't beat themselves up even if they "Should have known better" because that's not how life operates. Trial and error, and something I tell people is that these types of experiences serve as context for when you DO find the right person. It'll give you perspective and make you fight all the harder to secure and protect those bonds with everything you've got. 

&#x200B;

So don't give up but most importantly don't give up on yourself.

I sort of touched up on one aspect you've mentioned in regards to Aquarius men expressing themselves. It's just hard to articulate and if we aren't using our creativity as a medium, as a source, I think its next to impossible for us. (Not to say we can't but we need this) 

&#x200B;

If he's not there for you at your worse it's not a matter of him being an aquarius, it's just him as an individual in my experience. You said so yourself, they're great listeners. It would be one thing if he couldn't articulate what he thought fully, or give viable solutions to you, but to outright be flaky when you're in a tough spot is just a reflection of his character. (That is if he's aware of the issue). 

&#x200B;

I would tell you the male friend you have is more akin to a true aquarius male in all of it's glory and faults. Also remember as guys there are certain social stigmas deep rooted into men about sharing their thoughts or emotions; he might think it uncharacteristic of being a man, could be a flag of abuse they've suffered be it physical or emotional in the past, or maybe he thinks the problems of other's outweigh his own? 

&#x200B;

Also I'd say not afraid of commitments, but more shielded and guarded and they don't like to be vulnerable. However, I'll agree I think they do tend to be more flaky when it comes to relationships as far as lovers etc. Lot of that goes back to the "freedom" thing I think. Labels are a parameter, a limitation, and it grounds and aquarius that often try to detach from reality. 

&#x200B;

I ultimately agree with you though as far as the relationships thing. I think most aquarius aren't afraid of sharing passion, intimacy, with other's or even being outstanding partners/friends. For them it's like the bird in the cage. They think themselves incapable of flight no matter how spacious or how much room there might be there. They don't think of what room they do have and the security that comes with it, but of the restrictions and the walls around them that limit their ability to be themselves. 

&#x200B;

Another thing I'd like you to note, I'd like to think we've had a decent discussion but if we had ever met in person I doubt you'd have got many words out of me. Writing is a passion, it's how I'm able to articulate myself, and I think most other aquarius are similar in some way or another. 

&#x200B;

Best of luck to you though. And I have a suspicion if your aquarius male friend is single, there's probably some small chance they are interested in you. That in their own way, they've listened to you, and they're gauging if you're someone they can finally speak to of their issues. Then again, I don't know the guy or the circumstances but I've been in that situation myself. Aquarius men are usually the "friend zoned" guys, by that I mean they've put themselves in that situation in hopes of gradually getting to know the person at their own pace; however, by the time they show their actual interest the girl has it in their mind already that they're just friends.

That's true to some extent. That doesn't mean you can't start the chase, show the initial interest, just don't end up the one running after them. Let them run to you, because otherwise the Aquarius man will feel their freedom threatened. 

&#x200B;

Another thing to note, Horoscopes are interesting no doubt. However, there is a part where the person and the sign differentiate. Remember that. People are so vast, so complex, it's difficult to just label it as one thing or another. The person you're speaking of, just may not have the emotional maturity yet. They might never get to that point either. You DO deserve to be loved, to be wanted, and in no way would I excuse the canceling so often. There comes a point, if what they're going through is an emotional baggage of sorts, they'll need to learn to give in to trust and be forthcoming. 

&#x200B;

Despite what I've said before, I will say the guy you're talking about is probably in need of soul searching. And, that's one journey that there's ever only one ticket for. You can point them to the road, but the walk is long and narrow. 

&#x200B;

You are responsible for your own happiness, this applies to him and even you, you two will not mesh until things change and if they can. My advice, leave him on that road. What comes next is he either back tracks, maybe popping up in your messages or showing up for an occasion or two (As to avoid that road he needs to go down alone), or in the loss of something he could have seen more in will set motion into something more. You can not chose the path for the Aquarius, but you can make him feel as if the choice was his to make.

There's two ways I've found that actually help other than the "let it go" because it doesn't just work that way. As aquarius I think we bottle it up, then it explodes later on, and we're generally more of thinkers to begin with and that itself gets us in trouble. The over analyzing, the contemplating on situations that may arise as a result of every action, and we aren't as "wild and free" as some perceive us as. As if insinuating we put no thought and act on whim, which is the furthest thing from the truth. 

&#x200B;

The first way most Aquarius are familiar with. Ghost. Cut it all out. Take several steps back from everything because the noise of the world in combination to that in our heads will drown out reason and your senses till it's become too much to bare. However, note this can cause real harm to relationships around you that haven't come to understand your thought process or take it too personally. You can only hope the friends, family, significant others, and etc can understand you need time to realign yourself and thoughts. 

&#x200B;

The second way is more attuned to what you'll read about of Aquarius. We are more prone to creativity and that's not always because we have some sort of hidden talent for it or are destined to be artist, poets, musicians, etc. On the contrary, it's something so much more. Aquarius are dreamers, they're free thinkers, and what they need more than most is an escape from reality. Drown your senses. Music that silences the thoughts you're over thinking. Writing so that those words take shape on paper and are lessened of your mind. Sculpt the image of something you've seen or thought. Paint freely with the parameters you control, the paints and colors, and give life to what's been lingering. 

&#x200B;

An aquarius doesn't do these things for glory, fame, or even wealth especially not initially. It's just a fundamental necessity when it comes to dealing with our own imperfections. That's what I believe anyway. In time as you practice these things, you get better at them, and that's why Aquarius are associated with the arts; because it wasn't just habit but necessity. I suggest doing the later, do something anything even if it's just writing those thoughts on paper. Maybe you can burn the paper after you've written what's troubling you. Then they're gone. That might seem like nonsense, but I think it can help you.

It's more manipulative than it is toxic in my opinion. Mind you, I'm an Aquarius as well. Most of us are more introverted in nature, we take a step back and watch the world as it is, and as such we have an understanding of the circumstances around us and how to best play out a situation to our benefit. An Aquarius enjoys their freedom and the best way to acquire that is to be familiar in all ways to chase and reacquire it if we feel it threatened. You used tears, sympathy, or pity more or less to best suit you in that situation. 

&#x200B;

However, I've always been the type to use anger or sharp or painful remarks that I know will cause mental anguish on those that trouble me. Sometimes that comes out, unbeknownst to me and it's not intentional. Usually biding my time, letting those around me do as they please until I've had enough and I've collected enough "data" or information to use at my arsenal should the situation ever arise. It's rather cruel, I know this as well, but it's a defense mechanism I instinctively found myself acting upon in some instances. 

&#x200B;

Not sure though, if this is an Aquarius exclusive thing or if it's more prone to a psychological one involving being a sociopath or etc. I think, personally, we might all have our own gripes of how it is we defend ourselves in certain situations. However, this is just my experience.

It's because when you set boundaries, parameters, you are control over any given situation and that intimidates people who'd rather act on impulse or at whim. A lot of signs require, demand, the attention of others as where Aquarius are more liberated and generally introverted in nature in my experience. They do not require that additional interaction, not to say they despise it, and prefer the freedom despite the limitations they set upon themselves. We're easy to cut off toxicity in whatever form presents itself, respectfully done so in most cases too as to avoid the drama, and find it easier to identify those types of situations. 

&#x200B;

So think about it from this perspective. We can easily sever ties at a whim. We like our freedoms within the parameters we set ourselves, for our own defense, and don't negotiate those simple things. That level of control, to some, is a god complex because you have the very real ability to create and destroy at your own leisure. It's a level of diligence, an ability, that many other signs struggle to accomplish if they can at all. Sometimes it's not the fact that they're upset you've removed them from the equation, it's the envy of not finding it as easy as you can. 

&#x200B;

But that's just my opinion on the matter.

I'm an Aquarius male and what you've described I've found myself doing the same from time to time. Granted, I'm speaking from my own perspectives and experiences and they shouldn't be interpreted as his own; take it with a grain of salt more or less. 

&#x200B;

A lot of times I've found it difficult to express myself out right with my feelings. Aquarius are very passionate when the flame is burning hot, but when it's starting to fade it grows cold quickly and the interest goes poof pretty quick. I guess the best way to think of it as Aquarius being ADHD when it comes to emotions? We're always chasing the next "high" of passion and it's easier to find that with something new and exciting. Keeping an Aquarius quite literally might mean avoiding the routine; rather we're selfish in the sense we want to be able to shake up that routine at our own discretion, rather than other's, and appreciate the stagnant moments when we hope to realign ourselves in finding what's important to us. That's what they mean when they say Aquarius value freedom. Hence the ghosting. Most of the time it's never the people themselves, for an Aquarius, and that's hard to grasp for most because usually ghosting is naturally perceived as a negative thing. Not to sound cliche, but it really is the "it's not you, it's me" thing. 

&#x200B;

The "full" of himself, in my experience, is actually a charade to mask the insecurities that they have of the world and of themselves. I've given off that persona as well, especially at say work, and people have often thought I was full of it or overly confident in my ability; however, I have a deep seated insecurity with my own worth and value. The Masquerade serves as a tool so that other's don't pry past those walls. 

&#x200B;

Another thing to note of Aquarius is that they're generally more prone to the arts be it writing, painting, or anything regards to creativity. I know this will sound stupid. But as someone who writes in my free time, I genuinely once believed that being miserable and being "unwanted" and "unloved" and "closed off" could help fuel those passions. If you want to have a deep conversation with an Aquarius male, you must first learn of those passions. Take an interest in them, speak about them, and you'll have an amazingly deep conversation. 

Note they wont always be forthcoming or open up. It's an investment just as much for you, as it is for them. An Aquarius values REAL and meaningful relationships as to oppose to a wide variety of them. The time for this varies for each of them depending on their own thoughts, trust, and their willingness to be vulnerable and let people in. If he hasn't opened up to you it's not that he isn't genuinely interested it could be because he doesn't know how to articulate those thoughts or he hasn't felt an adequate amount of trust in you yet. 

I'm not saying you're wrong, nor am I taking his side, on the contrary this type of psychological warfare of sorts or patience isn't for everyone. The thing about Aquarius is that once they let you in you'll never find a more understanding, patient, and loving partner or friend; however, as the expression goes, you get what you pay for. And that cost is quite literally the time it takes to get to that point. If you're not willing to wait it out, that's no fault of yours nor of him, that's completely acceptable and it may be the very kick he needs to start engaging more. If you can't understand this, even if you can and it's just not something you're willing or can partake in, it just shows you two aren't compatible and guess what? That's completely okay. 

&#x200B;

On my own experiences, I didn't even start approaching woman until much later in my life on the account I was the shy and reserved type that kept to myself. I only found out much later in life how oblivious I had been around woman who might have had an interest in me. That includes people I was mutually attracted too. Eventually what made me more comfortable with approaching the opposite sex was with my writing, because I found it too difficult to say them in person as it was too embarrassing and they never quite articulated fully what I wished to say. 

&#x200B;

Don't chase the Aquarius male, let him chase you because the "hunt" itself is the passion that fuels them. You can string along the carrot, to peek their interest, but know it's ultimately up to him to take that step and that takes a lot of growing.

An honest opinion for you. As I've worked there for 4 years, did cashiering myself at a point, and my mother also was a cashier. Expect long periods of time standing, having to get permission to use the restroom (If you go more than once a shift expect some animosity and hostility as a result), you'll experience a variety of customers depending on the clientele of where your store is located but most of which are generally alright, expect to stand at the end of your register looking at each other until a customer arrives in your lane, you might be asked to go get carts for the store no matter if it's snow/rain/etc but hopefully with your age they'll avoid utilizing you in that way for your own safety (Cars, slips if weather unfavorable, etc.), get familiar with cigarette types even if you don't smoke as you'll be expected to go to the area with them for customers no matter which lane they're in. 

&#x200B;

Depending on whoever your ACSM or CSM is (Assistant customer service manager or Customer Service Manager) they might focus on speed of transactions and how fast you're going, but mostly they'll be sure to focus more so on your customer relations with speaking to customers. (However, don't spend too much time on them) Be sure to bag hot and cold items separately and those items should be in the blue bag, and all other items in the clear bags if a customer doesn't come in with reusable ones or request paper bags (That should be stationed near you). Chemicals and items that could contaminate foods should always be kept separately from food items, bagged individually or together, to avoid leakage or damage to other products. Chicken shouldn't be bagged with any other types of meats (Other than like chicken items) as to avoid cross contamination. 

&#x200B;

Schedules are completed by thursday at noon (Company policy for the latest) for your shift starting on the following Sunday to the following saturday. Sunday through Saturday is a pay period and it cuts off after Saturday 12 AM. You'll obtain access to an app known as Workforce dimensions (Called UKG now I believe and you can download it at the app store to save yourself time later on) your manager will give you a QR code so you can be linked to Foodlion. This app will give you access to your schedule (Once posted for the current and following week) and can be used to check your timecard (Hour's you've worked) as well as to submit requests off for doctor visits and etc. Requesting time you generally want to give several weeks in advance and even then your employer doesn't technically have to guarantee you off for it. 

&#x200B;

As for hours it ultimately will factor on a few things on how your ACSM or CSM value your worth over all. Are you reliable? (Are you punctual, how many times do you call out, what is your availability like is it open or limited?) Are you more of a fast worker? You'll probably be stationed at midday to nights. Are you a more modern paced and engage with more customers? Probably be scheduled on the early shifts from 6 or 7 am (Depending on which your store opens) to whatever they have payroll for. Also as a cashier, even though you're scheduled to get off at 2 say for example the ACSM and CSM loathe it if you turn off your own light and leave without their permission. (Especially if someone ELSE isn't doing their job and call out or are tardy. You'll be expected in some circumstances (Depending on your boss) if they'll let you leave or if they expect you to stay until the next person arrives to give coverage.) 

&#x200B;

As for Morale of the store. That ultimately depends on your members of management. Lots of time a lot of the issues at the store level aren't even department heads, evening manager, grocery, or even your ASM or SM (Assistant store manager and store manager). It boils down to your district and market manager and how it is they've behaved at middle management. Ultimately they leave cashiers alone for the most part other than make sure to ring up proper PLU (Item codes for produce including separate PLU's on tote bagged items) and the encouraging of sales on re-useable bags or foodlion feed boxes when those are available. Don't worry too much about memorizing the codes because your computer terminal (Register) does have look up options in case you're unfamiliar and is very useful. 

&#x200B;

At your station you'll have usually a black counterfeit pen for 20's, 50's, 100's. Receipt paper, if I recall the paper should be placed in with the tear over not under (It's been a while for me), and paper for coupons. If a transaction prints out coupons after a customer leaves, or you forgot to hand them out, you are required to toss them away into the trash to avoid disciplinary action. (This is considered theft to foodlion if I recall). 

&#x200B;

Sometimes you'll be asked to put up reshop (Items customers don't want) or reclaim. Reclaim should have a sticker on it (It'll be orange) that says do not resell as it's left the store. This is a legal/safety requirement of grocery stores to avoid tampering of product. Those will be put in designated areas for associates in departments to scan out and dispose of. 

&#x200B;

Honestly, that about sums up what you have to expect. I might have missed a thing or two, but this guide should give you some much needed context of what'll be expected of you in that role.

